## The Deep Insight

What you're saying, if I understand correctly, is that Fourier analysis isn't just *another technique* for time series—it's *why the whole enterprise works*. The back-shift operator B\mathcal{B} B is the fundamental object, and complex exponentials are its eigenfunctions. Any linear time-invariant operator inherits this eigenstructure. So when we decompose a signal into its frequency components, we're working in the natural coordinate system for LTI systems.

The "creation myth" is lovely: white noise (independent observations, flat spectrum) gets filtered through an LTI operator, and the resulting autocorrelation structure is the fingerprint of that filter. The spectrum tells you what the filter did; the ACF tells you how the present relates to the past. These are _two views of the same thing_, connected by Fourier transform.

This means:

|Perspective|Question|Natural Object|Emphasis|
|---|---|---|---|
|Time domain|How does past predict future?|ACF, PACF|Forecasting|
|Frequency domain|What structure generated this process?|Spectrum|Understanding the system|

And here's the connection to your philosophical theme: the time-domain approach emphasizes **decision support** (forecast the next value), while the frequency-domain approach emphasizes **understanding** (what periodic structures, what filtering, what physics). But they're mathematically equivalent—dual perspectives on the same underlying reality.

